  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 151 AM CDT Thu Jul 23 2026\n\nGenerally quiet weather is affecting the Northland early this \nmorning, with high pressure centered over southern Minnesota. We\nhave light west to southwest winds, and mostly clear skies \nacross the area. Some storms that developed earlier this evening\nover northwest Ontario where instability and shear were \nsufficient for storm development have been drifting southeast \ntowards the Arrowhead. However, as the environment is much less \nfavorable here, they have been weakening to little more than \nshowers as they cross the border. As we head through today, \nexpect mostly sunny skies and seasonable temperatures climbing \ninto the low to mid 80s. This afternoon and evening we will get \nadditional shower and storm development over northwest Ontario \nwhich may drift into the Borderlands. Have kept some small \nprecipitation chances going for these storms through the \nafternoon and evening. Light winds will gradually turn to the \nsouthwest and increase a bit by the afternoon. The overnight \nhours tonight will be quiet and mostly clear with comfortable \nlows in the upper 50s and lower 60s.\n\nFriday afternoon and night marks our best chance at showers and\nthunderstorms through this forecast period. A shortwave will \nmove across the flattened ridge over the western CONUS and \nacross the Northland Friday night. Ahead of it, southerly flow \nwill push in a noticeably warmer and more humid airmass, pushing\nhighs into the mid to upper 80s. A cold front which accompanies\nthe shortwave will approach from the northwest late Friday \nafternoon into Friday night. Instability and shear profiles \nahead of the front suggest a few elevated supercells could \ndevelop across northwest and north central Minnesota late \nFriday. These storms will carry a level two out of five risk for\nsevere weather, with large hail up to ping pong ball size and \nisolated damaging wind gusts being the primary hazards.\n\nBehind the departing front on Saturday, the predominant weather\nconcern shifts to heat. The upper ridge will amplify and shift \neast, allowing temperatures to soar into the upper 80s and low \nto mid 90s. With dew points remaining elevated, heat index \nvalues will climb into the 90s. If this trend holds, Heat \nAdvisories may be needed for portions of the Northland Saturday \nthrough Monday. Precipitation chances diminish for Saturday, but\nthen a mid level baroclinic zone and convergence draped across \nthe area may bring showers and thunderstorms to the area Sunday \nand Sunday night. These precipitation chances are somewhat \nconditional, as warm temperatures aloft may suppress any \nwidespread convection.\n\nThe unseasonably hot and humid conditions will persist into \nearly next week, with Monday likely seeing another day of \nwidespread upper 80s and low 90s across the area. A slight \nchance of showers and thunderstorms will linger Monday into \nTuesday, but confidence remains low on exact timing and coverage\nas most of the region remains capped under the strong upper \nlevel ridge and warm temperatures aloft. By the middle of next \nweek, the pattern suggests a slight moderation in temperatures \nback toward the 80s, but overall conditions are expected to \nremain warmer than normal for late July with relatively sparse \nprecipitation.",
